description:
ISO 7816-4 §5.1.3: SW 6C xx means 'wrong Le; correct Le is xx'. The card is saying: resend the same command with Le=sw2 and it will work. This is a normal, spec-defined response from real PIV tokens when the caller guesses the response length wrong.

wolfP11 currently returns WP11_PIV_ERR_SW on 6C xx, failing the operation instead of retrying.

Affected functions: wp11_piv_sign (line ~408), wp11_piv_generate_key (line ~784), wp11_piv_ecdh (line ~1070). wp11_piv_get_cert and wp11_piv_attest use GET RESPONSE chaining and are less likely to hit this.

Fix: In each function that constructs an APDU with a fixed Le, after piv_get_sw check:
  if (sw1 == 0x6Cu) {
      /* card is telling us the correct Le — resend once with Le=sw2 */
      cmd[cmd_len - 1] = sw2;  /* patch Le in-place */
      /* retransmit via ccid_apdu() */
      /* if 6C xx again, fail — avoid infinite loop */
  }
Limit to one retry to prevent infinite 6C→6C loops from a malfunctioning token.
Add comment: 'ISO 7816-4 §5.1.3: 6C xx means wrong Le; retry with Le=sw2'.
